<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Alesia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/52-bce">52 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Siege of Alesia · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> was the decisive siege of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>, fought in 52 BCE at the hill-fort of the Mandubii, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alise-sainte-reine">Alise-Sainte-Reine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> and his army with two lines of fortifications and beat off a huge Gallic relief army. Vercingetorix surrendered, ending organised resistance to the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alesia.jpg" alt="Battle of Alesia" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alise-Sainte-Reine_-_Contrevallation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 58 BCE Julius Caesar, after his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> of 59 BCE, was appointed proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, Illyricum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> for an unprecedented five years, with four legions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> of 58 to 50 BCE he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> beyond Narbonensis. In 53 BCE he announced that Gaul would be treated as a Roman province under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> and religion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> feared for the holy land watched over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a>, and the threat finally united them. Over the winter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, assembled an unprecedented coalition.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> avoided open battle and raided Roman foragers, hoping to starve the legions. Caesar took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaricum">Avaricum</a> by assault, but at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> suffered a clear defeat. After Caesar defeated Vercingetorix's cavalry at the Battle of the Vingeanne, Vercingetorix withdrew towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> was a fortified settlement of the Mandubii on a lofty hill with two rivers on different sides. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a> a direct assault was no longer attractive, so Caesar chose to starve the defenders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> sent out his cavalry to call a relief army. Within about a month the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built roughly 25 miles of works in two lines, one facing the town and one facing outward, with trenches, a moat, towers and hidden pits. Excavation suggests the lines were not continuous, as Caesar claims, but made use of the terrain.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> expelled their old, sick, women and children, but Caesar refused to admit them and they were left between the lines. The relief force arrived and attacked the outer works while the besieged attacked the inner ones, without success. Then 60,000 men under Vercassivellaunus struck a weak point on a hill the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> could not enclose. Caesar sent reinforcements under Labienus and joined the fight himself, while cavalry took the Gauls in the rear. The relief army fled. The next day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> proposed to the council that he be killed or surrendered, and he was handed over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> ended organised Gallic resistance, though southwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> was not yet pacified, and the Gallic territories north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> did not become a Roman province until 27 BCE. The Senate granted Caesar 20 days of thanksgiving but, for political reasons, refused him a triumph. Two years later, in 49 BCE, he crossed the Rubicon. Vercingetorix was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for five years, paraded in Caesar's triumph and ritually garrotted in 46 BCE.</p><p>The size of the armies is uncertain, since the only records are Roman. Caesar speaks of a relief force of a quarter of a million, probably an exaggeration, and modern historians usually think between fifty and a hundred thousand more credible. The site is most probably Mont Auxois above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alise-sainte-reine">Alise-Sainte-Reine</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> funded excavations there in the 1860s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Alesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pardoned after <b>Alesia</b>, they moved to the new city of Augustodunum (Autun), and in 48 CE Claudius admitted Aeduan nobles to the Roman Senate.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ambiani sent men to relieve <b>Alesia</b> in 52 BCE and fought again in 51 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ambiani">Ambiani</a></li><li><blockquote>In 52 BCE Vercingetorix raised Gaul in revolt and repulsed Caesar at Gergovia, but surrendered at <b>Alesia</b> and was later executed in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a></li><li><blockquote>Commius joined the great revolt of 52 BCE, helped lead the relief army at <b>Alesia</b> and later fled to Britain, where coins name his successors among the British Atrebates.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates">Atrebates</a></li><li><blockquote>They joined the great revolt of 52 BCE and sent five thousand men to relieve <b>Alesia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/aulerci-cenomani">Aulerci Cenomani</a></li><li><blockquote>They submitted in 57 BCE but sent men to <b>Alesia</b> in 52 BCE and joined the Bellovaci in 51 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/caleti">Caleti</a></li></ul></section>
